==English==

{{wikipedia|River Thames}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|Temese|lang=enm}}, from name for the river, {{etyl|cel|en}} ''Tamesas'' (from *''tamēssa''),<ref name=MalloryAdams147>Mallory, J.P. and D.Q. Adams. ''The Encyclopedia of Indo-European Culture''. London: Fitzroy and Dearborn, 1997: 147.</ref> recorded in {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|Tamesis|lang=la}} and yielding modern {{etyl|cy|-}} {{term|Tafwys|lang=cy}}. The name probably meant "dark" and can be compared to other cognates such as {{etyl|ga|-}} {{term|teimheal|lang=ga}} and {{etyl|cy|-}} ''tywyll'' "darkness" ([[Proto-Celtic]] *''temeslos'') and [[Middle Irish]] ''teimen'' "dark grey",<ref name=MalloryAdams147/> though Richard Coates<ref name=coates>Coates, Richard 1998 "A new explanation of the name of London", ''Transactions of the Philological Society'' 96, 2 pp.203–229 http://www.blackwell-synergy.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/1467-968X.00027; doi = 10.1111/1467-968X.00027</ref> mentions other theories: Kenneth H. Jackson's<ref>Jackson, Kenneth H 1955 "The Pictish Language" in F. T. Wainright (ed.), ''The Problem of the Picts'' Edinburgh; Nelson, pp. 129–166</ref> that it is non-Indo-European (and of unknown meaning), and Peter Kitson's<ref>Kitson, Peter R 1996 "British and European River Names", ''Transactions of the Philological Society'' 94, pp. 73–118; doi=10.1111/j.1467-968X.1996.tb01178.x|issue=2</ref> that it is Indo-European but pre-[[Celtic Britain|Celtic]] and has a name indicating "muddiness" from a root ''*tā-'', 'melt'.

Note also other river names such as [[Teme]], [[Tavy]], [[Teviot]], [[Teifi]].

The river's name has always been pronounced with a simple ''t'' /t/; the Middle English spelling was typically ''Temese''. A similar spelling from this era (1210 AD), "Tamisiam", is found in the [[Magna Carta]].<ref>Ellis Sandoz, ed. ''The Roots of Liberty: Magna Carta…''; Indianapolis; Amagi/Liberty Fund, pages 39, 347</ref>

